.TH EX 1 "September 19, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
ex \- edit text with a swap-backed line editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ex
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ex
is the root of a family of editors.
Display based editing is the focus of vi(1).
On maux, ex and vi share their line-editing engine; ex stores text in
a swap file so that the edited text need not fit in a process bank.
.PP
The buffer is a copy. Changes do not affect the original file until
it is written.
The basic addresses and a, i, c, d, p, r, w, s, g, v, m, t, j and =
commands are described in ed(1).
Type ex commands without vi's leading colon.
.PP
.B Differences from ed.
Use # or nu for numbered printing; n is reserved for next-file editing
and is not implemented.
The default r address is the current line, not end of buffer.
Join inserts a space between lines.
Bare , and ; mean the current line, not the whole buffer:
write 1,$ explicitly for every line.
A number after s/// is a count of lines, not an occurrence number.
.PP
.B Command names.
A command is named by its letters, as in POSIX ex, and may be
shortened to a prefix: append, change, copy (co), delete, edit,
global, insert, join, list, mark (ma), move, number (nu), print,
quit, read, substitute, undo, vglobal, write, xit, and the
one-letter k, t and z. Blanks may precede the name.
A word that is none of these is refused, so that cxyz is no longer
change: the message is
.RI `` word :
Not an editor command'' and nothing in the buffer changes.
A verb followed by an operand it does not take (2d3, 2=x, 1zx)
is refused with ``Trailing characters''. Counts, registers and
print flags are not implemented.
.PP
.B Additional commands.
.TP
.B q, q!
Quit, refusing unsaved changes unless ! is given.
.TP
.B wq, x
Write and quit; x writes only when modified.
.TP
.B w! file
Write, overriding the guard against overwriting a different existing file.
.TP
.B e file, e! file
Replace the buffer by another file, with or without the unsaved-change guard.
.TP
.B u
Undo the last change; another u redoes it. Undo is single-level.
.TP
.B kx, mark x
Mark the addressed line with letter x.
Use 'x as an address and '' for the previous-context mark.
.TP
.B ?pattern?
Search backward, wrapping around. ?? and // reuse the last pattern.
.TP
.B l
List text with nonprinting characters made visible.
.TP
.B z
Print a window of text.
.TP
.B &
Repeat the last substitution.
.TP
.B !command
Run a shell command without changing the buffer.
.TP
.B range!command
Replace the addressed lines by the command's output.
.TP
.B r !command, w !command
Read command output into the buffer, or write the addressed text to a command.
.PP
For example,
.B 1,$p
prints the buffer and
.B 1,$s/old/new/g
replaces every occurrence.
Shell commands use sh(1), including its real pipelines and its limitations.
.PP
The session uses EX.SWPxx in the working directory.
Shell escapes also use temporary command/data files there.
Quit normally to allow cleanup.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/EX  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ed(1), vi(1), sh(1), licenses(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Normal completion returns 0; recorded command/file errors cause status 1, and initial working-area failure gives 2. Script-mode errors may be silent: inspect the exit status, not just visible question marks. Interactive ex prints the refusal message; vi shows it, or ``Command failed'' for an error with no message of its own, on the status line until the next key.
.SH BUGS
The line table holds 2048 lines; input lines/commands are bounded to 250 bytes and edited line descriptors to 254. Global commands have at most 250 pending matches. There is no full Berkeley option system, tags, multi-file next list, crash-recovery interface or transition from ex into visual mode. Start vi separately.
